# Model roles — v5.0.0 Phase D0 (five role classes; was three in v4.0.0/V1).
#
# Maps each workflow ROLE to a selection STRATEGY. model-select.sh reads
# this on SessionStart and rewrites every agent's `model:` pin per its
# role, so different classes of agent can ride different model tiers while
# each class still auto-adopts the newest model in its lane.
#
# Format
# ------
# Simple `key=value` lines. Comments (`#`) and blank lines are ignored.
# Whitespace around the `=` is tolerated. Same shape as .claude/rubric-config.
#
# Roles (fixed set — model-select.sh's ALL_ROLES)
# ----------------------------------------------
#   designer         the design agent (designer.md).
#   design_reviewer  the independent design reviewer (design-reviewer.md).
#   orchestrator     the planning/delegation agent (orchestrator.md).
#   implementer      the writing agents (backend.md, frontend.md, devops.md).
#   reviewer         the read-only review agents (qa.md, grader.md, judge.md).
#
# Strategies
# ----------
#   top             the resolver's single best pick — newest model in the
#                   most capable family in the listing (Fable-class today),
#                   exactly the v3.5 behavior. Honours .claude/model-ranking
#                   exclusions.
#   <family>-class  the newest claude-<family>-* model in the listing.
#                   `opus-class`, `sonnet-class`, `fable-class`, `haiku-class`
#                   and any family shipped in the future all parse from ONE
#                   rule (`^[a-z][a-z0-9]*-class$`) — there is no per-family
#                   enum to extend. Selection is deliberately NOT gated on the
#                   family appearing in .claude/model-ranking, so a brand-new
#                   family is usable on day zero. Ranking exclusions still
#                   apply INSIDE the subset; an empty or fully-excluded subset
#                   warns and falls back to `top`. When the family is also
#                   absent from the ranking tiers the warning adds "not a
#                   known tier — check for a typo", which is how `sonnnet-class`
#                   is told apart from a genuinely unlisted family.
#
# A missing file, a missing key, or an unrecognised strategy value all
# resolve to `top` (fail-open) — setting every role to `top` reproduces
# v3.5 single-pin behavior exactly. Keys this file does NOT carry are
# reported in the resolved artifact's `missing_keys` array and surfaced as a
# SessionStart warning: this file is manifest class `operator`, so an install
# whose copy was EDITED receives the new defaults as a `.claude/model-roles.new`
# sidecar and keeps running the old key set silently. `missing_keys` is what
# makes that visible instead of silent.
#
# WHY THE DESIGN LANES ARE `top` AND NOT `fable-class`
# ----------------------------------------------------
# `fable-class` would say what we mean today and be wrong tomorrow. Pinning a
# lane to a family FREEZES it to that family: pick_best ranks an unrecognised
# family in the TOP capability class, so `top` adopts a newly-launched tier
# above Fable on day zero, while `fable-class` would keep the design lanes on
# Fable until somebody edits this file. `top` resolves to Fable today AND
# auto-adopts. Ship `<family>-class` as legal grammar; do not use it here.
#
# Per-unit escalation (a strategy, NOT a role)
# --------------------------------------------
#   implementer_class_high=<strategy>
#     The strategy a single unit of work escalates to when the design artifact
#     declares `implementer_class: high` for it (mechanised as the Beads label
#     `impl-class-high`). It owns no agent files and NEVER appears under
#     `roles` in the resolved artifact — it is resolved through the same
#     role_strategy + pick_for_role path and recorded under `escalation`.
#
#       model-select.sh escalate <task-id>   # pin the implementer lane up
#       model-select.sh restore              # put it back
#
#     Escalation is a DECLARED, AUDITED, REVERSIBLE pin change and nothing
#     more. Whether the runtime honours a mid-session frontmatter `model:`
#     change is not established anywhere in this tree and is not verifiable
#     offline, so no test, doc or release note here claims that an escalated
#     unit RAN on the escalated model.
#
# Reviewer lane / design-reviewer lane (optional)
# -----------------------------------------------
#   reviewer_lane=auto|claude
#   design_reviewer_lane=auto|claude
#     auto    (default) let the workflow detect a review lane via
#             .claude/scripts/codex-detect.sh — Sol via Codex when present,
#             Claude otherwise.
#     claude  force the Claude path regardless of any probe.
#   WORKFLOW_REVIEWER_LANE / WORKFLOW_DESIGN_REVIEWER_LANE override their key
#   (test/operator seam). Reviewer and design-reviewer agents ALWAYS carry a
#   Claude `model:` pin in frontmatter — never the frontmatter pin, and a lane
#   never blocks the session.
#   THE TWO LANES ARE NOT SYMMETRIC, despite the symmetric key names:
#     reviewer_lane        REAL. codex-review.sh drives the Sol code review, so
#                          this lane genuinely changes which reviewer is engaged
#                          and the statusline renders the literal `sol` for it.
#     design_reviewer_lane RESOLVED AND REPORTED, BUT NOT DRIVEN. Nothing
#                          executes design review through Codex, so this lane
#                          changes neither the reviewer nor (since
#                          claude-workflow-plugin-yvpe) the statusline, which
#                          always renders the design reviewer's resolved Claude
#                          id. Its only remaining effect is the one documented
#                          as a defect below: it suppresses the identity-collapse
#                          flag.
#
# IDENTITY COLLAPSE — and the ONE way to clear it
# -----------------------------------------------
# `designer` and `design_reviewer` are both `top` by default, so on a stock
# install WITHOUT Codex they resolve to the same model and the design would be
# reviewed by its own identity. That is reported, never blocked: the resolver
# sets `identity_collapse: true` in the artifact, writes
# .claude/.qa-tracking/design-family-collapse, warns at SessionStart, and the
# statusline shows `!id`. Pins are still written and the exit status is 0 —
# blocking here would make the Codex-absent arm unrunnable.
#
# ONE REAL CLEARANCE, and one that only LOOKS like one:
#   1. Set `design_reviewer=opus-class` (or any family-class distinct from
#      whatever `top` resolves to). The two lanes then resolve to different
#      models on the Claude path alone. THIS IS THE ONLY ONE THAT WORKS.
#   2. DO NOT RELY ON INSTALLING CODEX. It was documented here as a clearance
#      and it is not one (claude-workflow-plugin-yvpe). Installing Codex makes
#      design_reviewer_lane resolve away from `claude`, and the collapse
#      condition below requires (identical pick AND lane == claude) — so the
#      flag goes out while BOTH ROLES STILL RESOLVE TO THE SAME MODEL. No
#      script drives design review through Codex: codex-review.sh is the
#      CODE-review driver and contains no design handling, and
#      .claude/agents/design-reviewer.md instructs the reviewer to emit
#      `design-claude` unconditionally because it has no tool to read this
#      lane. Taking this "clearance" therefore leaves you strictly worse off
#      than not taking it: the risk is unchanged and the warning that would
#      have told you is gone.
#
# RIDER (v5.0.0): implementer moved from opus-class to sonnet-class. That is a
# deliberate quality-for-cost trade the reviewed design artifact and the
# green-to-green per-unit tests are meant to absorb, with per-unit escalation
# as the safety valve. Track grader rounds per implementation task before and
# after; if rounds rise materially the revert is the ONE line below
# (`implementer=opus-class`) and the observation belongs in LESSONS.md.
#
