#
# Correction 7 does NOT cover either row, and it is worth being exact about
# why: it licenses `top` for the DESIGN lanes because Fable IS the top
# family there, so `top` resolves to precisely what the plan asked for
# while still adopting a newer top family on day zero. Opus is not the top
# family, so `top` on these two rows was a HIGHER tier than the plan
# specified — a deviation, in the safe direction, named here rather than
# left to be inferred from the file.
#
# WHY BOTH WERE DEFERRED IN D0: D0 shipped no working designer (D1) or
# design-review loop (D2), so the ORCHESTRATOR SEAT was still where design
# judgment happened, and `reviewer` is the gate's own seat — demoting
# either before its replacement/safety-net existed would have removed
# capability from the surface doing (or catching) that work.
#
# REVIEWER LANDED EARLY (2026-09-07), ahead of its own deferral. The
# deferral reason didn't survive contact with the operator's actual
# standing instruction: QA and review run on Sol in priority to Opus, and
# NEVER on Fable (a weekly-limit constraint). `top` resolving to Fable was
# silently violating that instruction on every reviewer spawn — Fable was
# never a legitimate seat for this role, so the capability trade the
# deferral was protecting did not apply here. `reviewer` shipped
# `opus-class` (the directive's own fallback tier) without waiting for D4.
#
# ORCHESTRATOR LANDS HERE (2026-09-18, fkm.9 / D7), also ahead of its D4
# deferral, for the SAME weekly-limit reason at HIGHER volume: `top`
# resolved to Fable, and the orchestrator is spawned on essentially every
# non-trivial turn — far more often than any reviewer — so it was the seat
# burning the protected weekly Fable budget hardest, not the seat the
# deferral was protecting. DISCLOSURE, so this can be challenged rather
# than inherited: the operator's instruction as literally stated names QA
# and review, not the orchestrator; extending it here is an inference from
# the same weekly-limit rationale, made explicit rather than assumed. The
# directive's own D0 table is not an inference — it independently
# specifies latest Opus-class for this row on its own terms, and D1/D2
# (the design lanes) are now shipped, so the capability-removal concern
# above no longer applies either. Re-checked at this landing: the
# identity-collapse condition is unaffected (it compares `designer`
# against `design_reviewer`, never `orchestrator`), and the statusline
# group render still degrades correctly through more than three distinct
# groups — see `.claude/scripts/tests/model-roles.test.sh` section 4
# (4.7-4.12 cover the grouping/truncation mechanism generally; it is
# strategy-agnostic, so no new case was required for this specific
# role-value combination).

designer=top
design_reviewer=top
orchestrator=opus-class
implementer=sonnet-class
reviewer=opus-class

implementer_class_high=opus-class

